  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy through sundown, then mostly clear and\ndry with decreasing winds tonight through Saturday.\n\nIt's been a partly sunny day but also quite breezy with a mixing \ndepth to 800 mb, supporting westerly wind gusts in the 25 to as \nmuch as 35 mph this afternoon. Expect those conditions to\ncontinue through sundown, bringing a decrease in gust speeds as \nthe mixed layer decreases. The main issue for tonight is if\nwinds can slacken enough to support patchy frost. A sprawling\narea of high pressure centered over western NY/PA is forecast to \nmove eastward overnight tonight but not really crest over\nSouthern New England until Saturday morning. Efficient\nradiational cooling is expected tonight; there may be just\nenough of wind (around 5 mph) to keep frost from developing or\nin isolated coverage, and for those reasons haven't issued \nfrost headlines for those non-Northwest MA zones where the \ngrowing season continues. If you do have crops or plants, it may\nnot be a bad idea to consider taking protective actions to \nprevent frost damage, but I think the coverage ends up being too\nisolated. Lows forecast in the mid/upper 30s in the coolest \noutlying areas similar to surface dewpoints, and into the low to\nmid 40s for the urban areas and coastlines. \n  \nA chilly start to Saturday but high pressure becomes established \nacross Southern New England. Much of the day features full or \nmostly-full sunshine and light northwest winds, but a canopy of\nhigh-level clouds/cirrus will be streaming in from the south \nand southwest. Seasonable highs in the mid 60s under full sun. \nHigh pressure then retreats eastward by Saturday night, \nsupporting lows in the upper 30s to the mid/upper 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next quick moving round of rain comes Sunday \nafternoon and evening, continuing overnight and exiting Monday \nmorning.\n\nThe latest guidance suite continued to exhibit large uncertain\nwith the timing of rainfall ending Monday morning. The\ndeterministic GFS was on the more progressive side, with many of \nthe international models more closely aligned with a slower\ntiming and closer track to southern New England. However, there \nis agreement on the overall trend of drier conditions developing\nthrough the day. It is the timing that will need to be refined \nwith later forecasts. For now, held on to a chance of showers \ntowards the Cape and islands for a time Monday morning.\n\nThe timing will also impact rainfall totals. At present,\nexpecting this will be a modest rainfall, with rainfall amounts \ngenerally between one half to one inch. A slower timing, should \nit occur, opens the door for rainfall up to 1.50\", mainly\ntowards the Cape and islands.\n\nA brief dry period is anticipated for Monday afternoon and most\nof Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Quick round of rain around mid week, then dry \nweather returns by Thursday.  \n\nOur region should then get clipped by a pair of low pressures,\neach taking a different track. The first should pass by to our\nnorth later Monday night into Tuesday. Its cold front likely \ndoes not clear the coastal waters until late Tuesday night. This\nwill be a period of more showery precipitation.\n\nAnother dry period is expected Wednesday though most of Thursday \nnight, with high pressure moving across northern New England. \n\nThe forecast for next Friday is uncertain. A low pressure near\nJames Bay could swing its associated fronts across our region \nFriday into Friday Night. Have a mention in the forecast for \nmore showers some time Friday. However, this timing could be \ndelayed with later forecasts.",
